    {
        "content": "<p>Consider the recursively defined function</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">reducible</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>There is a yellow squiggle under <code>reducible</code> saying that <code>marking functions defined by well-founded recursion as </code>reducible<code> is not effective</code>. If I use instead <code>implicit_reducible</code>, the yellow squiggle disappears, but as far as I can tell the function doesn't get the attribute either. Shouldn't it be there also in this case?</p>",
        "id": 576982508,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1772546300
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That’s probably an oversight.</p>\n<p>Note that you can get a reducible definition if you use a <code>Nat</code>-valued measure:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">reducible</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n<span class=\"n\">termination_by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sizeOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Whether it has the reducibility properties you are looking for I don’t know :-)</p>",
        "id": 576985535,
        "sender_full_name": "Joachim Breitner",
        "timestamp": 1772547027
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't really care about reducibility. It's just that a similar example in Mathlib tripped a linter I'm writing, because the definition was marked <code>@[implicit_reducible]</code> but the linter complained it wasn't, so I was a little bit confused.</p>",
        "id": 576988804,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1772547759
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>With the termination measure you're giving, <code>reducible</code> works fine, but <code>implicit_reducible</code> doesn't (i.e., the definition can't get the attribute). This doesn't look meaningful to me...</p>",
        "id": 576991477,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1772548399
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks, reported as <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/issues/12775\">https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/issues/12775</a></p>",
